The SSE decoder in src/sse.rs is extracted and modified from
litellm-rust/crates/framer/src/sse.rs. The streaming transport follows
litellm-rust/crates/framer/src/framed.rs. Claude request/response and usage
normalization are adapted from litellm-rust/crates/llms/src/anthropic/chat/
transformation.rs and litellm-rust/crates/core-utils/src/core_helpers.rs.
Compatible-provider parameter mapping follows litellm-rust/crates/llms/src/
openai_like/chat/transformation.rs.

Modifications remove the surrounding workspace and platform dependencies,
restrict official providers to OpenAI, Claude and Grok, retain an optional Workbuddy
test provider, introduce a standalone HTTP client, and implement
tool, image and streaming support missing from the upstream Rust chat path.

The standalone HTTP server, authentication, configuration, multi-deployment
scheduler, retry/fallback logic, circuit breakers, limits and in-memory usage collection
are newly implemented in Rust for this project.

OAuth ports added from the same LiteLLM revision:
- litellm/llms/chatgpt/{authenticator,common_utils}.py and responses/transformation.py
- litellm/llms/xai/oauth.py and its Chat/Responses integration
- litellm-rust/crates/llms/src/anthropic/common_utils.rs (Rust extraction)
- litellm/completion_extras/litellm_responses_transformation/transformation.py
The ChatGPT default instruction text is retained in src/oauth/chatgpt_instructions.txt.
Selected upstream authentication test cases were adapted into Rust tests.

UPSTREAM.json records ported functions, integration changes and validation.

Automatic model discovery is a new Rust integration using provider REST schemas,
LiteLLM model-list/OAuth header behavior and
the Codex model endpoint/query schema. It does not copy Codex implementation code
or a static model catalog. Pinned protocol references are recorded in UPSTREAM.json.

In-memory state/credential injection/CLI integration is new teellm code, not an upstream LiteLLM port.

Online administration, validated configuration generations, provider compatibility
profiles, account health, session affinity, shared account limits,
bounded resource handling, management checkpoint/restore and operational diagnostics
are new teellm Rust integration code. OAuth algorithms retain the provenance above.
Declarative per-operation request header/query/JSON/form configuration is new
teellm code, applied after the retained provider transformations. It includes no
script interpreter or replacement OAuth/streaming protocol implementation.
Official-host enforcement, raw model routing with separate pool selection, and
lossless reasoning-parameter bridging/protection are new teellm Rust integration.

The private teerouter-egress component links unmodified Xray-core v1.260327.0
(upstream release v26.3.27), https://github.com/XTLS/Xray-core, under MPL-2.0.
Source is available at https://github.com/XTLS/Xray-core/tree/v26.3.27.
The helper and Rust pipe/SOCKS lifecycle integration are new teerouter code.
Exact transitive versions and checksums are recorded in egress/go.mod and go.sum.
Container distributions include dependency notices and the Go license under
/usr/share/doc/teerouter-egress; the helper is part of the measured router image.

Error/interruption-only text estimation uses unmodified tiktoken-rs 0.12.1
(MIT), https://github.com/zurawiki/tiktoken-rs,
revision 72a5a800651a1cea1ad609292446ba02c80e3bcd. The bundled tokenizer
assets originate from OpenAI tiktoken, https://github.com/openai/tiktoken
(MIT). Their license texts are in LICENSES/tiktoken-rs-LICENSE.txt and
LICENSES/tiktoken-LICENSE.txt. The bounded evidence collection and estimate
billing policy are new teerouter code; estimated counts are not provider usage.
